  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/3/35/Strange_Tales_Annual_Vol_1_2.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The Invisible Girl helps Johnny train in their backyard of Glenville, Long Island.&nbsp; When they go in, Johnny is disgusted to find an issue of <i>LIVE</i> magazine devoted to Spider-Man.&nbsp; He's hot that Spider-Man gets credit when he does something, but when the Torch does, it's the Fantastic Four that gets all the news.\n\nMeanwhile a monocle-wearing crook known as the \"Fox\" (whose name is Reynard Slinker, as revealed later in the issue) eyeballs a newly-discovered Da Vinci masterpiece in a museum.&nbsp; After cutting off all electrical power to the museum, he and his men pose as electrical repairmen and lift the painting while \"fixing\" the damage, making sure to leave behind a bit of spider web to ensure that Spider-Man gets the blame.&nbsp; Sure enough, the press reports that Spider-Man stole the painting.\n\nWe find the real Spider-Man musing about how he'll have to catch the real thief to prove his innocence.&nbsp; Thinking he could use some help, and knowing that the Human Torch lives in Glenville, he thinks it would be good to work alongside another teenager.&nbsp; He arrives at the house just as Torch receives a phone call from the \"chief,\" asking if he'll help find Spider-Man.&nbsp; Johnny is then surprised when he looks out the window and sees him right there!\n\nEven though Spider-Man came in good faith, he finds himself suddenly being attacked by the Human Torch, giving him little choice but to fight back.\n\nWhile the Torch pauses a moment to recharge, Peter ducks into a chemical lab to work on a form of webbing that will hold the Torch.&nbsp; When he confronts him next, he tries to explain to the Torch that he was framed, but Torch is having none of it.&nbsp; Spider-Man senses that the Torch only wants headlines and tells him so, taking off -- exactly what Johnny was accusing <i>him</i> of earlier!&nbsp; As Spider-Man leaves, Torch realizes his mistake and heads to the police to have them call off the hunt for Spider-Man.&nbsp; While there, the police chief determines that the real culprit seems likely to be Reynard Slinker (alias) the Fox.\n\nJohnny decides that working with Spider-Man wouldn't be such a bad thing, now that he has an actual lead, and he leaves a sky-written message for Spider-Man, asking that the two of them work together.\n\nSpider-Man sees the message and shines his spider symbol from the top of the Statue of Liberty.&nbsp; Torch shows Spider-Man a photo of the Fox, saying that his inspector friend suspects him.&nbsp; They encounter the Fox in an underground subway station.&nbsp; They find an underground hideout, but no Fox, so Spider-Man uses his spider-sense to lead them to New York's \"Bowery,\" and there, under a wooden Indian statue, they find some of the Fox's men, who tell them Fox is headed for Central Park.\n\nSpider-Man's senses lead them to a hidden lair under a water fountain.&nbsp; They finally track down the Fox posing as a peddler, selling views of the moon and stars through a telescope for a dime.&nbsp; The painting is rolled up in the telescope.&nbsp; They lead the Fox off to jail.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | StockyBuild | width=12%] As Steve Ditko had already had a couple of opportunities to draw the Human Torch (over in [LINK:1962:ASM_1_asm1|ASM #1] and [LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3]), it seemed like this 18-page treatment as part of this year's annual gave Jack Kirby his chance to try his hand at Spider-Man, which he does here for the first time.\n[BR]\n[BR] Kirby's rendition of the character is stockier and a bit bulkier than Ditko's drawings, but I did not find it distracting at all.&nbsp; Spider-Man also struck me as a bit shorter, but that might just be an illusion caused by the stockiness of the renderings.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Or that Kirby has a tendency to draw certain characters awkwardly, as he does with Iron Man -- a couple of examples being the cover of AVEN #4 and page 12 of TTA #82.]&nbsp; Ditko was on hand as inker to assist in the rendition, no doubt.\n\n[ASSET: left | ClosedEyes | width=11%] And speaking of drawings, there were many close-ups of the Human Torch's face in this issue, and it seemed like his eyes were closed in every one...\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(What's with <i>that</i>?)]\n\nStan uses the publicity given to the solo act of Spider-Man as the catalyst to cause irritation on the part of Johnny, who feels the wall-crawler gets all the headlines, while his own activities are merely dismissed as being only one of the Fantastic Four.&nbsp; (I guess Johnny doesn't read the <i>Daily Bugle</i>, where the headlines there are far less complimentary).\n\n[ASSET: right | WireCutters | width=8%] Narrative shortcuts were to be found aplenty, such as the single wire on page 3, which, when cut by nothing more than what seems like a simple, hand-held pair of wire-cutters, kills all \"electric power within a three-block radius.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=Seriously?]\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Convenient that such a wire is near enough to street level that someone can just walk up to it and... <i>snip</i>!)]\n\nBut even though the painting heist was portrayed as <b><i>way</i></b> too easy on the part of the Fox and his men, it's understood by the readers that it was all for the purpose of staging the head-to-head battle between the Human Torch and Spider-Man.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And yet, with a little more care, Stan and Jack might have crafted a bit more believable catalyst.)]\n\nThe theft and framing of Spider-Man were rushed so completely, that by page 4, when Spider-Man finally appears, there is never once any thought of why everyone suspects him.&nbsp; No, this is all very much in the vein of \"Oh, I've been framed!&nbsp; Everyone believes I'm a criminal!&nbsp; I must get help from the Human Torch!\"\n\nIt's all so very artificial, especially given how Spider-Man already has been portrayed as getting bad press (at least by the <i>Bugle</i>).&nbsp; Even [LINK:1962:ASM_1_asm1|ASM #1], showing Spider-Man's first encounter with the Fantastic Four goes out of its way to include the line, \"Besides, aren't you wanted by the police?&nbsp; This isn't 'Outlaws Anonymous'!\"&nbsp; Bad press has been part of Spider-Man's shtick from the very beginning.\n\nYet none of this seemed to matter to Stan and Jack, who seemed bound and determined to race things along, all to get to the actual conflict between the two teenaged-heroes, as if the issue was late for an appointment.&nbsp; (Don't get me wrong -- I <i>get</i> it -- the battle between the Human Torch and Spider-Man is why people were buying the magazine -- it's all there on the cover, after all -- but still... you have to admit the setup here was not only a bit on the weak side, but was also dismissed quickly in order to feature the action.)\n\nDuring the initial battle between the two heroes (pages 5-8), it almost seems at times that Spider-Man has super speed (see a couple of panels on page 6, for instance).&nbsp; Later, on page 8, we read about his \"blasted spider vision.\"&nbsp; At first, these new abilities seem out of character here, but it could simply be Johnny's <i>interpretation</i> of his spider-powers (because as author, Stan Lee certainly should have <i>known</i> better).&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: We know that years later, in ASM #77, Spider-Man uses Johnny's lack of understanding of his spider-powers to get rid of him using his \"Spider-Sonic Hearing\" -- <i>Hahahaha</i>!]&nbsp; At least we get a taste of Peter Parker's already-established science skill on pages 8-9, when he whips up a special web formula that later aids him in his battle against the Torch.\n\nIt does indeed work to stop the Torch long enough for the wall-crawler to explain why he's come, but when the ever-hot-headed Johnny doesn't seem predisposed to listen, Spider-Man heads off alone.\n[ASSET: center | ForgetYou | width=67%]\n\nSpider-Man repeatedly shows his superior ability to work alone, and the reader never gets the sense that the Human Torch has much of a chance against him.&nbsp; I understand he's the <i>guest-star</i>, and so there may have been a little deference to him in the plot, but let's also not forget that the Torch is part of a team, which means he's used to relying on others to help him get out of jams, unlike Spider-Man, who is always on his own.\n\n[ASSET: left | ItsTheFox | width=12%] Johnny's later visit to the police chief on page 12 serves two purposes.&nbsp; First is to clear Spider-Man's name, and then to point Johnny in the direction of the Fox as the <i>real </i>culprit.&nbsp; (Again, this is nothing other than a narrative shortcut and one that is not entirely convincing.)&nbsp; But it at least gives Johnny a way to save face and suggest that the two of them work together instead of fighting.\n\nI have to say that I liked that the spider-signal was used this soon after its initial appearance two months ago (in [LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3]), as we see on pages 9 and 13, though I do wonder how Spider-Man was shining it into the air from the top of the Statue of Liberty.&nbsp; What was he shining it on?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I'm guessing we're just not supposed to ask these questions.)]\n\nThe rest of the book was nothing short of yawn-worthy, and the likelihood of the Fox having his secret lair under the tracks in a subway station -- [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=Come on!].&nbsp; It was as if the story had reached the end of anything remotely approaching compelling.&nbsp; The two heroes had battled, and then later met on the Statue of Liberty.&nbsp; From that point on, it was formulaic... and honestly... dull.\n\nThe Fox posing as an old lady, only to hand Spider-Man something sticky so that the Torch would have to burn it off for him?&nbsp; I ask you -- where was Spider-Man's spider-sense <i>then</i>?&nbsp; (Conveniently forgotten for the sake of the plot, it seems.)&nbsp; But it comes back in time to lead the pair of them to the Bowery on page 16.&nbsp; <i>Presto</i>!&nbsp; Another of Fox's secret lairs!&nbsp; (How many does this guy <i>have</i>?) Which only leads them to Central Park... and yet another secret lair beneath a water fountain.\n\nVery quickly, the book began to lose what little credibility it had, as if Stan and Jack were only going through the motions because they needed the two of them to work together.&nbsp; The villain is captured, the painting recovered, and the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved.]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt was nice to see the two teamed-up for their first extensive outing together (they'd only briefly met twice before, both times in the pages of <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i>).&nbsp; Also nice was the <i>consistent</i> way that Spider-Man's reputation was so suspect that Johnny and the police believed he <i>might</i> actually be guilty.&nbsp; But the plot didn't really give them much to do after they were done facing off against each other, so I am mostly disappointed.\n\nIt was sort of an unofficial first Marvel Team-Up between Spider-Man and the Human Torch, at least one that lasted more than a few panels.&nbsp; Just like Johnny and Ben in the FF, their constant bickering was obviously just a smoke screen to hide their tenuous mutual respect.&nbsp; (Even the Fox comments on this in the last panel, begging them to lock him up \"so I can stop <i>listenin' </i>to you two nuts!\")\n[ASSET: center | LockMeUp | width=67%]\n\nBut I do agree with Spider-Man on the last page -- Johnny seemed along mostly for the ride.&nbsp; It was Spider-Man's spider-sense that ultimately led him to the Fox, this story's cardboard cutout of a villain, there solely to fill space and serve as a foil for the two heroes to catch -- one made to look like some clever, master criminal with several secret hideouts, but one that fooled no one.\n\nOther than one scene when Peter ducks into a laboratory to use science to help him stop the Torch though, he seemed to be particularly out of character, mainly in the first half the story -- the latter half was a bit better, as he was far more confident in his abilities.&nbsp; But there was nothing at all remarkable brought to the table here, and this tale is likely to be soon forgotten, not quite rising to full reader status.\n\nIt plodded a bit, particularly in the latter half and was at times a chore to read, but since I'd already started it, I wanted to see how it would end.&nbsp; But that's about as nice a thing as I can say about this largely staged meeting that had more hype than sizzle.",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * Small detail though it is, we learn on page 2 that Sue and Johnny Storm share a \"modern split-level home in Glenville, Long Island.\"&nbsp; Later, on page 4, Glenville is described as \"a suburban town on Long Island's south shore.\"&nbsp; In my [LINK:1962:ST_101_torch0|ST #101] review, I already directed readers to this location:&nbsp; [CITATION: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glenville,_New_York] for where you could read more about the actual town, but now we're being told it is part of Long Island.\n[BR]\n[BR] Not to make a mountain out of a molehill, it seems that Glenville, Long Island is a <i>fictional</i> place now, which <i>does</i> seem to contradict its location implied in that earlier issue, but also helps explain why it <i>would</i> be possible for the submarine to have appeared there, as shown in that issue.&nbsp; <i>Ah well</i>...\n\n[HANG0] * Page 3 shows copies of the <i>Daily Chronicle</i>, apparently a Glenville publication which we had seen before (in [LINK:1962:ST_101_torch0|ST #101]).&nbsp; Still, a headline such as [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=SPIDER-MAN STEALS A PRICELSS PAINTING] would seem more likely as part of a <i>Daily Bugle </i>issue.\n\n[HANG0] * When Spider-Man arrives at the home of Sue and Johnny Storm, there's a Fantastic Four symbol in the middle of the lawn.&nbsp; That seemed unlikely, unless it was mowed into the grass by one of the suburban town's residents as a joke.\n\n[HANG0] * The final panel that indicates the <i>Fantastic Four</i> annual is \"on sale now\" -- is a lie.&nbsp; It was not published until early July, 1963 (and becomes part of that month's batch of issues, which I'll get to in due course)."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "Anyone familiar with the loose partnership between Spider-Man and the Human Torch realizes that meeting atop the Statue of Liberty becomes their \"thing,\" and this is merely the first of many meetings between the two of them that takes place here.&nbsp; (For one of many examples, see the cover of 1980's SPEC #42.)\n\nI do still wonder how Spider-Man actually gets out to Liberty Island (obviously the Human Torch just flies there), but readers can likely safety assume he's able to leap from one boat to another out in the harbor as he makes his way out there.\n[ASSET: center | StatueOfLiberty | width=50%]",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}